  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.2. Increased institutional capacity of workers&apos; organizations</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">In efforts to improve services to attract new members or retain existing ones, the Malawi Congress of Trade Unions (MCTU) facilitated several training workshops for workers in the informal economy at five technical colleges: Salima Technical College, ECOM Vocational Training Institute and Comboni Technical College, Lilongwe Technical College and Don Bosco Youth Technical Institute. A total of 801 students were trained (32.4 per cent or 260 were women).

Each of the technical colleges focused on a particular trade including carpentry and joinery, painting and decorations, electrical installation and electronic, plumbing and bricklaying, to promote life long learning.

MCTU has been attracting new members through the newly established organising committee which adopted an organizing plan. The committee used the existing regional and district structure of MCTU to reach out to particularly workers in the informal economy.  This has resulted in contributing effectively to the formulation and coherent implementation of economic, social and environmental policies at the national level.

In addition, through the mobilization and awareness raising of workers in the tea and coffee sector in Malawi, MCTU has been promoted decent work and  increased respect of fundamental principles and rights at work. In turn, this facilitated the promotion of tripartite and bipartite social dialogue to address the pressing issues in this industry. In March 2021, the ILO provided input to the discussions for evaluation of decent work in the informal economy. The ILO input was based on ILO Recommendation No. 204 (2015) that calls for application of policies and labour laws for workers in the informal economy. These concerns were incorporated into the final design document that guided MCTU during the implementation of their intervention to provide lifelong skills to their members. 185 students out of the 801 particpants were co-financed from the Decent Work and Informal Economy project.</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.2.1. Number of national workers&apos; organizations with innovative strategies to attract new groups of workers and/or to improve their services</narrative>
    </title>
